{
  "result": "After the rain, the city seemed to have been freshly invented, its rooftops shining like dark boats moored beneath a silver evening sky without warning.\n\nPuddles gathered along the curb, holding upside down versions of streetlamps, apartment windows, and the hurried faces of people returning home beneath their umbrellas again.\n\nA boy in a yellow coat sailed a paper boat through the gutter, steering it past cigarette ends and a bruised red maple leaf nearby.\n\nHis grandmother watched from the bakery door, one hand resting on a warm loaf, while the bell above her head chimed whenever customers entered slowly.\n\nNobody hurried; even buses moved carefully through the flooded intersection, carrying bright advertisements and tired passengers toward neighborhoods softened by the weather and twilight too.\n\nOn balconies, plants lifted their leaves, collecting the last drops as if the storm had delivered a message worth keeping through the long night ahead.\n\nFrom an open window came a piano, tentative at first, then steady, threading a melody between sirens, dripping gutters, and distant train wheels in motion.\n\nPeople paused beneath awnings, listening without admitting they were listening, each secretly grateful for a reason to stand still just a little longer than necessary.\n\nBy dusk, the clouds had broken apart, revealing a narrow strip of peach colored light above the roofs on the western edge of town again.\n\nTomorrow, everyone would remember errands, deadlines, and damp shoes, but tonight the city belonged to small wonders no one needed to explain to anyone else.",
